Manual review corrections for this page's first pass.

- "$12$ ft$^{2}$" and "$480$ m$^{3}$" split the quantity and its unit
  across two math spans instead of typesetting the unit as \text{} inside
  one span, unlike the unit convention used throughout the rest of the
  repo (e.g. "$4\,\text{ft}^{3}$", "$2\,\text{g}/\text{cm}^{2}/\text{yr}$");
  changed to "$12\,\text{ft}^{2}$" and "$480\,\text{m}^{3}$". The per-area
  costs "\$5/m$^{2}$" and "\$3/m$^{2}$" had the same defect plus an escaped
  dollar sign left outside math mode; changed to single math spans
  "$\$5/\text{m}^{2}$" and "$\$3/\text{m}^{2}$", matching how currency is
  kept inside math elsewhere (e.g. "$\$1000$").
- "$x\,\text{and}\,y$" and "$x,y,\,\text{and}\,z$" used \text{and} with
  manual math spacing to join lists of variables instead of keeping "and"
  as ordinary prose outside math mode; changed to "$x$ and $y$" and "$x$,
  $y$, and $z$", matching the convention used elsewhere in the repo (e.g.
  "$m_{1}$ and $m_{2}$").
- "dimensions $x,y$" had no prose space after the comma; changed to
  "dimensions $x$, $y$".
- The bare numeral "1.0" in "the sum of ... is 1.0" was left as plain
  prose even though it is a mathematical quantity; wrapped in math mode
  as "$1.0$".
- Five exercises used a semicolon inside one math span to separate the
  objective function from its constraint (e.g.
  "$f(x,y)=x^{2}y;x^{2}+2y^{2}=6$"), while every other exercise in this
  same list uses a comma for the identical construct. Split four into
  two comma-joined spans ("$f(x,y)=x^{2}y$, $x^{2}+2y^{2}=6$"); the fifth
  ("Maximize $\begin{array}{l}f(x,y)=x^{2}-y^{2};x>0,y>0; \\
  g(x,y)=y-x^{2}=0\end{array}$") has its constraint inside a
  \begin{array} nested within one outer math span, where inserting a
  second math delimiter would break math mode, so that semicolon was
  swapped in place for a plain comma instead of being split into a
  separate span.

